 "schema_version": 1,
 "note": "The god catalogue terrain_religion.py loads, extracted from FantasyWorldGenerator b67c99b after lint_catalogue() passed. TWO FILES ARE MERGED HERE AND THE SPLIT IS LOAD-BEARING. `gods`, `cosmologies`, `affinities`, `affinity_weights`, `fear_gods`, `wild_push_families`, `fold_free_cosmologies`, `aspect_midpoint` and `nest_aspect_push` come from pantheon.json; `hidden_gods` and `hidden_notes` come from hidden_pantheon.json. terrain_religion.catalogue_identity() hashes ONLY pantheon.json and stamps that digest into every generated world, which is exactly why the four Outside gods were given a file of their own: folding them into the hash would retire every saved world whenever one was touched. A port must reproduce that same scope -- `source_identity.sha256` below is the digest of the known half alone, and `hidden_source_identity.sha256` the separate digest of the hidden half. Both are sha256 over json.dumps(doc, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(',',':'), sort_keys=True), so they do NOT depend on this file's key order. ORDER IS A CONTRACT within `gods` and `cosmologies`: resolve_gods() and hidden_records() emit one record per row in list order into the world document, and choose_cosmology() builds its weighted draw by walking `cosmologies` in order and subtracting from a single uniform draw, so reordering is a different world on the same seed. A hidden god carries `school` (singular); a known god carries `school_affinity` (a list) and, if its family is 'school', the school name again inside `exists`. The two are not the same field and hidden_records() maps `school` to a one-element `schools` list on export. `affinities` keys are civilization ids and must cover every id in entity-rules.json exactly; `epithets` keys are civilization ids too. `race` (on the three ancestor gods) and the `race` inside an existence rule are PARENT RACE ids from registry-sections.json, not civilization ids. `fear_gods` keys are nest FAMILY names from nest-profiles.json; `wild_push_families` maps a god id to the nest families that push it toward its wild aspect. `aspect_midpoint` and `nest_aspect_push` are compared against mean leyline intensity times (1 + instability), which is unbounded above -- they are not fractions. Existence rule kinds terrain_religion.rule_holds() accepts: all, always, any, civilization_city, diaspora, dragon, gods, magic_disabled, magic_enabled, nest_family, ocean_share, primordial_count, race, races, ruins, school, sky.",
